# Workflow contract (generic)

These rules bind every automation and every agent that builds one with the
`workflow-*` skills. The signal vocabulary lives in
`starter-kit/signal-contract.md`; do not invent new headers or labels without
adding them there first.

## Human gates

- Nothing starts implementation without an explicit human signal (`/approve`, a
  label added by a human, or an approved ledger row).
- Follow-up triggers (`/ask`, `/clarify`, `/refine`) fire on comments **by Me /
  allowlisted humans only**, never Anyone. The single exception is the QA wake on
  "PR opened" by the bot account.
- Merges are always human. No automation merges, force-pushes, or deletes branches.
- Builder skills propose files; a human approves before rules, hooks, workflows,
  or automations are written or installed.

## Reply surface

- If a PR exists or the trigger came from a PR comment, reply on the PR.
- Otherwise reply on the issue (or the ledger row for product intake).
- Never post the only copy of an answer or a fix summary on a different surface.

## Unclear or not feasible

- Unclear: comment `## NEED CLARIFICATION` with one question per line, mark
  `needs-human`, and STOP. Do not open a PR.
- Not feasible: comment `## NOT FEASIBLE` with `Why`, `Tried/blocked`, `Options`,
  mark `needs-human`, and STOP. Do not invent a workaround.

## Verdicts and loops

- QA posts exactly **one** verdict comment per wake, with an exact header as its
  first line (`## QA PASSED`, `## QA FAILED`, `## QA SKIPPED`, `## QA BLOCKED`,
  `## QA PASSED WITH CONCERNS`).
- Build/lint/test is a **gate**, not a verdict. Dev never claims QA passed.
- Fix is capped at 3 iterations. On the cap, comment
  `Max iterations reached (3 attempts). Requesting human review.`, mark
  `needs-human`, STOP.
- After a fix: push to the same branch, comment `Fix applied: <one sentence>`, and
  re-signal QA with a label pulse (remove then add `qa-rerun`).

## Labels

- Labels are attached by a deterministic router (GitHub Action or equivalent) from
  exact comment lines, not by the agent, unless the repo explicitly grants it.
- Router attaches use remove-then-add so label-added webhooks always fire.

## Branches and commits

- Branch `exp/<issue-number>-<short-slug>` off the default branch.
- Commit after each logical sub-task / acceptance criterion with a conventional
  message (`feat(scope): …`, `fix(scope): …`).
- Check off acceptance criteria in the PR body as they are satisfied.

## Read-only by default

- Monitoring, discovery, and measurement are read-only toward GitHub, trackers,
  and sheets.
- Never commit secrets; use the user's existing CLI/MCP auth.

## Measurement

- Every automation must emit the signals above so the monitor and
  `python -m framework` can observe it. A stage with no signal is a measurement
  gap, not a pass.
- Trust numbers follow `factor-provenance.mdc`: only CLI output, no invented
  factors or values.
